---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Safety | HerbGuide"
  description="Important safety notes, sourcing guidance, and plain-language boundaries for educational herb content."
  structuredData={[
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "WebPage",
      name: "Safety",
      description:
        "Important safety notes, sourcing guidance, and plain-language boundaries for educational herb content.",
      url: "https://herbguide.store/safety/",
    },
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are kitchen herbs always safe because they are foods?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Food-level use is often gentler, but quantity, concentration, product quality, medications, and personal context still matter.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does HerbGuide recommend herbs for illness?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. HerbGuide explains traditional use, kitchen context, and safety language. It does not diagnose, treat, or replace medical care.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell narrow prose-block">
      <p class="eyebrow">Safety</p>
      <h1>How to read herb content responsibly</h1>
      <p>
        HerbGuide is an educational resource. It shares traditional context, general kitchen
        ideas, and research summaries, but it does not replace personal medical care.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not provide diagnosis, treatment plans, or personalized medical advice.</li>
        <li>Traditional uses are described as traditional uses, not as proven cures.</li>
        <li>Pregnancy, nursing, medication use, and chronic conditions all require extra caution.</li>
        <li>Every herb page should include a caution block before the site launches publicly.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Read carefully</p>
        <h2>Traditional use is not the same as a treatment claim</h2>
        <p>
          When a herb page says an ingredient is traditionally used in TCM to support qi,
          nourish blood, moisten dryness, or clear heat, that is a description of how the
          herb is discussed inside a traditional system. It is not a promise that the herb
          is appropriate, effective, or safe for every reader.
        </p>
        <p>
          That difference matters. Good educational herb content should help readers
          understand context, not push them into self-diagnosis.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Higher-risk situations</p>
        <h2>When extra caution matters most</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Pregnancy or trying to conceive</li>
          <li>Breastfeeding</li>
          <li>Prescription medication use</li>
          <li>Blood-thinning medication or clotting concerns</li>
          <li>Autoimmune conditions, organ disease, or complex medical history</li>
          <li>Children, older adults, or people with multiple ongoing health concerns</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Chinese herbs safety guide for beginners</h2>
        <p>
          A food-style ingredient in soup is not the same thing as a concentrated extract,
          powdered supplement, tincture, or formula blend. Source, dose, preparation, and
          product quality all affect how a herb should be understood.
        </p>
        <p>
          HerbGuide will generally favor educational content around traditional food use,
          whole herbs, and careful terminology. That keeps the site aligned with its
          practical, low-hype editorial standard.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block list-card">
        <h2>Are Chinese herbs safe to take without guidance?</h2>
        <p>
          If discomfort is persistent, severe, worsening, or confusing, readers should not
          rely on blog content alone. The right next step may involve a physician, a
          pharmacist, or another qualified professional depending on the situation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site can help readers ask better questions. It should not pretend to replace
          real evaluation.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Safety SEO</p>
        <h2>Food therapy safety, herb sourcing, and medication caution</h2>
        <p>
          This page is also designed to support searches around Chinese herbs safety,
          herb-food interactions, beginner kitchen-use safety, and whether kitchen-use
          herbs are automatically safe for everyone. The answer is no: context still matters.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Trust signal</p>
        <h2>Why a safety page helps the whole site</h2>
        <p>
          Clear safety language improves user trust, advertising readiness, and search
          quality. It also gives every herb page a stronger foundation, because readers can
          see that HerbGuide is educational first, not a shortcut to self-treatment.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell two-up">
      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reader questions</p>
        <h2>Herb safety FAQ</h2>
        <h3>Are kitchen herbs always safe because they are foods?</h3>
        <p>
          No. Food-level use is often gentler, but quantity, concentration, product quality,
          medications, and personal context still matter.
        </p>
        <h3>Should I use one herb page to decide what to take?</h3>
        <p>
          No. Herb pages are educational summaries. They are useful for learning terminology,
          common food uses, and caution notes, but not for personal decision-making.
        </p>
        <h3>Does HerbGuide recommend herbs for illness?</h3>
        <p>
          No. HerbGuide explains traditional use, kitchen context, and safety language.
          It does not diagnose, treat, or replace medical care.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="prose-block card">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keep exploring</p>
        <h2>Safety pages work best with herb pages</h2>
        <p>
          Once this page makes sense, go back to the
          <a href="/herbs/">Herb Library</a> and read caution sections on specific herbs
          like <a href="/herbs/goji-berry/">Goji Berry</a>,
          <a href="/herbs/licorice-root/">Licorice Root</a>, and
          <a href="/herbs/ginseng/">Ginseng</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          For gentler learning paths, continue to <a href="/glossary/">TCM Glossary</a>,
          <a href="/topics/balanced-pantry-habits/"> Balanced Pantry Habits</a>, and
          <a href="/food-therapy/"> Food Therapy</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
